      "question": {
        ":question/id": "3e659ad0-30de-54df-a737-29fc87668c32",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/problem": "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n$\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{10 x^{3}}{5 x^{4} + 4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =5 x^{4} + 4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =20 x^{3} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =20 x^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{d}u & =x^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5 x^{4} + 4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 4 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{10 x^{3}}{5 x^{4} + 4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{4}^{9}\\frac{10}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{4}^{9}\\frac{10}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int_{4}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\ln(u)|_{4}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\ln(9)-\\ln(4)) \\\\  & =- \\log{\\left(2 \\right)} + \\log{\\left(3 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
