        ":question/problem": "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n$\\int_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2 x^{3}}{2 x^{4} + 9}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =2 x^{4} + 9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =8 x^{3} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =8 x^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}u & =x^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2 x^{4} + 9.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 11 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2 x^{3}}{2 x^{4} + 9}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{11}^{9}\\frac{2}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{11}^{9}\\frac{2}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{11}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\ln(u)|_{11}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\ln(9)-\\ln(11)) \\\\  & =- \\frac{\\log{\\left(11 \\right)}}{4} + \\frac{\\log{\\left(3 \\right)}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
